  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 225 PM EDT Monday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Seasonably warm temperatures and comfortable \nhumidity through Wednesday. Mainly dry outside of a stray shower \nnear the Albemarle Sound.  \n\nIt's another beautiful late August day in the Mid-Atlantic. \nDewpoints are in the 50s pretty much everywhere and temps are in the \nmid 80s under mostly to partly sunny skies. There is a stationary \nboundary laying right along the Albemarle Sound, which has already \ntriggered a few showers/storms to the south. Based on the lack of Cu \nand lower dewpoints on the north side of the sound, confidence is \nlow that any of those showers make it into the FA. Still cannot rule \nit out, though, so kept the 20-30% PoP along the far south through \nthe early evening. The dry air already in place, light north winds, \nand mostly clear skies overnight will allow for another cool night. \nWent a bit below guidance for the lows to account for this, yielding \nlows in the upper 50s in the NW and the inland rural areas and low \nto mid 60s elsewhere. The sfc high remains in place through early \nWednesday, so expecting a very similar day tomorrow. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... Unsettled conditions return mid to late week with \ndaily chances for showers/storms. \n\nGlobal models indicate that the sfc high slides offshore sometime \nWed/Thurs and the flow aloft shifts back to the SW. Wednesday still \nlooks like a decent day, but the dewpoints will climb back into the \n60s with the return of southerly flow. Still mostly dry on Wed, but \nevening showers/storms could creep into western portions of the area \nbased on latest guidance. As of now, Thurs and Fri look like the \ndays for most widespread storms when another front comes through. \nNot too sure about the risk of severe now that chances for morning \nprecip have increased on Thurs and the flow aloft will be weakening \ngoing into Fri. If precip can hold out until the afternoon Thurs, \ncould certainly see a few stronger storms.",
